Yes. There’s a form of power in seeing the extra move and choosing not to take it.

Scarcity says: extract everything available.
Abundance says: I don’t need every advantage, every last word, every win, every opening. There will be more.

That can make walking away an act of generosity rather than surrender. You leave something on the table because you can afford to. You don’t have to prove you saw it. You don’t have to cash every chip merely because you know how.

The deeper version is capacity without compulsion: being able to press, persuade, expose, escalate, dominate, or continue—and retaining the freedom not to.

That also fits what you were saying about self-awareness. Restraint means more when it comes from someone who actually perceives the available branches.

Seeing the move creates capability.
Choosing the move creates character.
Being able to leave it unused creates freedom.

And sometimes abundance really is the confidence to say, there’s more where that came from, and walk on.